[NAME]
buckle\-libinput \- Nostalgia bucklespring keyboard sound, libinput flavour

[=DESCRIPTION]
Buckle emulates the sound of an old IBM Model-M space saver
bucklespring keyboard while typing.
.PP
It runs as a background process and plays back the sound of each key
pressed and released on the keyboard, just as if using an IBM Model-M.
The sound of each key has carefully been sampled, and is played back
while simulating the proper distance and direction for a realistic 3D
sound palette of pure nostalgic bliss.
.PP
This flavour reads key events straight from the raw input devices in
.I /dev/input
through libinput, rather than from X11, so that it also works under a Wayland
compositor and on the console.  Those devices are not readable for ordinary
users out of the box, and running it as root is no answer, as it then no
longer reaches the sound daemon of the user session.  The udev rule installed
with this flavour,
.IR 70\-bucklespring\-libinput.rules ,
grants that access to the user at the local seat, and is inert until armed;
read the comments in it for how, and for what it costs you.
.PP
libinput discards the key repeat the kernel generates for a held key and
leaves repeating to its caller, so this flavour keeps its own clock.  Nothing
can tell it when a repeat has actually happened: a Wayland compositor sends
no event per repeat, it tells each client the delay and the rate once and
every client then runs a timer of its own, so the repeats that put characters
on the screen exist only inside whichever window has the focus.  What can be
had is the delay and the rate themselves, and buckle asks in turn the
compositor, which is authoritative under Wayland, then the input device,
which is authoritative on the console, falling back on the kernel default of
250 ms and then one click every 33 ms.  Should it still disagree with what
you see on the screen, set it with
.B \-\-repeat\-delay
and
.BR \-\-repeat\-rate .
Modifier keys never repeat.  Use
.B \-r
for the older behaviour, a single click however long the key is held.
.PP
The mouse clicks too: the buttons, and the scroll wheel, one click per detent.
Two fingers scrolling on a touchpad make no sound, having no detents to make
one for.  Use
.B \-\-no\-click
to silence it, or to silence only some of it.  Reading the mouse
needs access to the pointer devices as well as the keyboard ones, which the
shipped udev rule grants together.
.PP
The X11 flavour is
.BR buckle\-x11 (1).

Where it was built with the system tray, and there is a tray to put it in, an
icon appears there.  Its menu holds the two things worth one click, pausing
and quitting, and an entry that opens the preferences window; rolling the
wheel over the icon changes the volume without opening the menu at all.
.B \-\-no\-tray
leaves the icon out.  It is a StatusNotifierItem, which KDE Plasma shows as it
is and GNOME Shell shows through an extension; where nothing is listening
there is simply no icon and nothing else changes.
.PP
The volume, the audio device and the headphone filter are in that window, and
not in the menu: a menu on a tray icon travels over dbusmenu, which carries
labels and check marks rather than widgets, so a volume there can only be a
ladder of radio items and a device list has to be rebuilt as a submenu
whenever it changes.  It also means a desktop with nowhere to put the icon
still has all of it.  The running
.B buckle\-libinput
puts the window up when it is asked:
.B \-\-preferences
asks over the session bus and exits; that second invocation plays nothing and
holds no sound device of its own.  The desktop file carries the same thing as
an action, named
.IR "Bucklespring preferences" ,
and
.B SIGUSR1
does it too, for a keyboard shortcut with no dbus tooling in it.  Closing the
window leaves the clicks running.

Each key is played at a position of its own, left to right as it sits on the
board, which
.B \-\-stereo\-width
scales.  On headphones
.B \-\-hrtf
runs that through OpenAL's head related transfer functions, which is the
difference between a key sounding placed somewhere around you and merely
panned between your ears.  OpenAL left to itself does this only when it
believes it is talking to headphones, and
.B \-\-hrtf=auto
is that default; the preferences window offers the same three choices while
running.

[SEE ALSO]
.BR buckle\-libinput.service (8),
.BR buckle\-x11 (1),
.BR update\-alternatives (1)

